1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:10,671
In the Encyclopedia of Triangle Centers, the Incircle and three Excircles are the four tritangent circles tangent to all three sidelines of triangle A B C.

2
00:00:10,671 --> 00:00:19,930
The interior angle bisectors concur at the Incenter X one, while the exterior angle bisectors meet at the three excenters J A, J B, and J C.

3
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:29,076
The incircle has radius r equal to the area Delta divided by the semiperimeter s, touching the sides at the intouch triangle T a, T b, T c.

4
00:00:29,076 --> 00:00:39,120
Seven of the nineteen centers in Collection One—X one, X seven, X eight, X nine, X ten, X eleven, and X twelve—are generated directly by the tritangent circles.

5
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:54,060
Connecting the vertices to the incircle touch points gives the Gergonne Point X seven; connecting them to the excircle touch points gives the Nagel Point X eight; and the incircle's tangency with the nine-point circle defines the Feuerbach Point X eleven.
